**Read:** [Intermediate Fluid Mechanics — James Liburdy](../sources/Intermediate-Fluid-Mechanics-Liburdy.lingua#r_45KKWKB7DTN9MMQ951P62JHH5N), viscous scaling and boundary-layer development; [Advanced Fluid Mechanics — MIT 2.25, Fall 2013](../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Mechanics-MIT-2-25.lingua#r_1ZVEYBFSRC755ASSQ2X87MD8VZ), dimensional-analysis topic and problems; [Microfluidics: Fluid physics at the nanoliter scale — Squires and Quake](../sources/Microfluidic-Physics-Squires-Quake.lingua#r_5TN8JGKY1B6VKGJQG859J61JE2), complete dimensionless framework.

Choose a characteristic length L, speed U, time and material state from the actual question. Scaling momentum with inertial stress ρU² introduces Re=ρUL/μ; gravity, surface tension, sound and rotation produce other ratios. A different dominant balance may call for a viscous pressure scale μU/L instead.

Read each dimensionless coefficient as a comparison of terms, not as an automatic flow classification. Reynolds number depends on the stated length and speed. A pipe diameter, boundary-layer thickness and particle radius describe different questions. A low-Reynolds-number outer flow does not justify dropping every time derivative without a timescale comparison.

Nondimensionalize a chosen equation yourself, including boundary and initial data. Identify terms that are retained, neglected and potentially important in a smaller region. Predict which measurements or simulations would distinguish those approximations. Keep the dimensions of the dimensional inputs visible throughout the calculation.

